Bryan's Meet and Learn notes: office hours, and a picture of a class

His last round, and the shortest — two notes, one per page. Both are built.
**Neither is visible on the site yet**, and that is the thing worth reading
this PR for rather than a caveat at the end of it.

## What he asked for

**Meet.** *"I'd like to see office hours posted on the page."* With a
sub-bullet: *"If we have a board directors section, we can post each board
member's office hours in their mini-bios."*

**Learn.** *"Only note, picture on the page of people teaching classes."*

## What is in here

- `office_hours` per person in `_data/board.yml`, rendered inside the roster
  card on `/meet/`, above the bio.
- `photo` per person in the same file — which has been *documented* since the
  file was written and was never rendered by `meet.md`. An optional field that
  silently does nothing is worse than no field, because the first person to set
  one concludes the site is broken rather than the template.
- `photo:` (`src`, `alt`, `caption`) in `_data/classes.yml`, rendered at the
  top of `/classes/`.
- Round 2 written up in REVIEW-NOTES, CONTENT-TODO updated, and two CSS rules.

## The finding: this renders nothing today

`_data/board.yml` is an empty list and has been since it was written. So a
field for each board member's office hours currently produces zero board
members' hours, and the president has asked for something that cannot appear
until somebody types the roster in.

That is not a reason to hold the PR — the field has to exist before the content
can go anywhere — but it does mean **"Board and staff roster" has quietly
changed category.** It was under *never existed and probably should*. It is now
a named person waiting on a named thing, and I moved it in CONTENT-TODO to say
so. A name and a role is enough to publish someone; hours, bios and photos can
follow per person.

Same shape on Learn: the photo slot is real, the photograph does not exist.
That one joins the pile already waiting on your Google Drive pull and Bryan's
shoot — with a brief attached now, which the others don't have: *people
teaching*, not the room and not the gear.

## One judgement call you should check

*"People teaching classes"* has two readings: **one photograph of instruction
happening**, or **a portrait of each instructor** beside their class. I built
the first.

Reasoning: it needs one file rather than a roster of instructors the site does
not model at all — `_data/classes.yml` sessions have no `instructor` field
today — and if he meant the second, the first is thrown away cheaply rather
than being in the way. But it is a guess about someone's intent and it is
answerable by asking him one question, so I have not treated it as settled.
It's logged as open in REVIEW-NOTES round 2.

## Two smaller decisions, stated so they can be overruled

- **No studio-wide office-hours field.** He tied hours to people, and an
  org-level one would be a second place to look and a second thing to
  contradict. If the studio ever keeps hours belonging to nobody in particular,
  `org.yml`'s `address.note` already says "Open by appointment" and is where
  that goes.
- **`alt` is required, not optional.** A `src` with an empty `alt` is not
  rendered at all. These are photographs of identifiable people doing a
  specific thing — the exact case where a decorative empty `alt` is wrong — and
  a guard holds better than a comment asking nicely.

## Verified

Ruby works locally now, so this is checked by running it rather than by
reading it.

- `bundle exec jekyll build` clean.
- **Empty states**: no `card-portrait`, no `office-hours`, no `page-photo` in
  the built output, and `/meet/` still shows the roster TODO.
- **Populated states**, via temporary fixtures since no real data exists: a
  member with photo + hours + bio renders all three in order; a member with
  only name + role renders no stray markup; the Learn photo renders with its
  caption.
- **The alt guard**, deliberately: `src` set with `alt` empty produces no
  `<figure>` at all.
- Fixtures removed and the build re-run to confirm the empty states came back.
- Full Playwright suite, 575 passing.

## Unrelated, but you should know

`main` is red and it is not us. All three of last round's merges failed the
smoke job at `playwright install --with-deps`, because Google's apt repo served
a `Packages.gz` whose hash didn't match a `Release` file republished minutes
earlier. I re-ran it once; it failed identically. The tests themselves pass —
I ran the same 575 locally against merged `main` and they're green.

It should clear when Google's mirrors converge. If it doesn't, the fix is to
drop the `google-chrome.list` apt source before installing, since we use
Playwright's bundled Chromium and never Google's Chrome — but that's a change
to CI to work around somebody else's outage, so I'd rather wait a few hours
than ship it reflexively.
